.TH CTPOOL_SUBMIT_FUTURE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_submit_future \- submit a task that returns a void* result (blocking)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_future *ctpool_submit_future(ctpool pool,
                                    void *(*fn)(void *),
                                    void *arg,
                                    ccol_retval_t *rv_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a future object and submits a task to the pool. The task
calls fn(arg) on a worker thread and stores the void* that fn returned
in the future, where ctpool_future_get can read it later.

On success the future has a reference count of 2: the caller holds one
reference, and the queued task holds the other inside the library.
Each ctpool_future_get call that runs on the future adds one more. The
caller must call ctpool_future_free exactly once when it is done.

With a bounded queue the function blocks until the queue has a free
slot; use ctpool_try_submit_future or ctpool_timed_submit_future if you
don't want it to block.

A call from a task running on this same pool, or from the on_complete
callback of that task, while a bounded queue is already at capacity,
fails with ccol_container_full instead of waiting, for the reason
ctpool_submit(3) gives: only the workers of this pool can free a slot,
and the caller is one of them. An unbounded queue never gets into that
case.

When rv_out is not NULL the function stores a result code there on
every return, so a caller can tell the reasons for a NULL return
apart. The codes are the ones ctpool_submit(3) returns for the same
conditions.

A future always uses the standard malloc and free, never a custom
allocator that the caller set when creating the pool.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool    Thread pool handle
fn      Task function returning void* (must not be NULL)
arg     Passed to fn (may be NULL)
rv_out  Receives the result code on every return (may be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool_future handle on success, and stores
ccol_success in *rv_out.
Returns NULL on failure, and stores one of these codes in *rv_out:
  ccol_invalid_args       pool is CTPOOL_INVALID, or a stale and
                          already destroyed handle, or fn is NULL
  ccol_not_permitted      the pool is in shutdown
  ccol_container_full     a bounded queue is at capacity and the
                          caller is a worker of this same pool
  ccol_not_enough_memory  a memory allocation fails
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_submit (3),
.BR ctpool_try_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_timed_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_future_get (3),
.BR ctpool_future_done (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
